[#:seon.task{:name "a-refused-settlement-is-stored-as-a-core-fault"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A run whose settlement is refused by a declared writer refusal is stored as a core fault (signature 6e0c6d4e..., seon.db/transact!), shows 'Contract finding: seon.db/transact! returned this error ...' and carries a datahike.writer stack trace into the MCP reply, although it is the agent's mistake. Decide it in evaluate-sources, where the run's working value already settles each form's entity: an entity settled-value refuses becomes that form's refusal through refuse-install, and settle-batch-refusal!'s ordinal search is deleted."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/evaluate-sources seon.turn/settle-batch!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/fix-dogfood-api-2026-09-26.md"
                         "docs/seon/issues/a-refused-run-settlement-replaces-every-forms-shown-result.md"
                         "docs/seon/issues/expected-refusal-logs-raw-datom-error-twice.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "refusal-schemas-are-guessed-by-key"
             :kind :function
             :intent "refusal-terminal-data (and the settle-batch! refusal and provider-failure recordings: three copies) chooses an error schema with a ~15-arm key-presence cond, because producers attach no :seon.error/declared-schema: seon.db write refusals and invalid-read, seon.schema validation, reply no-forms, render walk/request/ambiguous/invalid-output/unknown, transcript, config, fn namespace-unresolvable, eleven seon.ai refusals, cluster.prompt and turn rules. A missed producer gave a nil schema and panicked root's graph at every boot (fixed for one producer in e2d1690d4). One scripted sweep adds the metadata at each producer, then the three conds go."
             :must-read #{seon.error/recording seon.turn/refusal-terminal-data}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/fix-root-refusal-schema-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-preview-reparses-retained-source"
             :kind :function
             :intent "The preview path (system-turn with write? false, used by the page) re-parses retained source through planned-sources and parses root's retained bare symbol 'accepted' as no forms, so the whole preview is refused. The write path does not re-parse."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/planned-sources seon.turn/system-turn}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/fix-root-refusal-schema-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "close-turn-is-a-third-commit"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A replied run commits close-turn as a separate third transaction after settlement: a trivial root run costs 122-136 ms warm, about 75 ms of it three file-store commits (24-31 ms each), and close-turn is 32-36 ms. Fold the close into the settlement at the writer (hunk in the fix-write-cost landing), keeping the separate pass for issue-bearing agents and generated system turns."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/close-turn seon.turn/settle-batch!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/fix-write-cost-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-reload-between-forms-is-undetected"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A reload between the forms of one run, or between admission and settlement, is undetected; only a reload inside one evaluation is (call-detecting-program-change since e81cb51dc). Wrap the run's evaluate-and-settle in call-detecting-program-change with since captured at admission."
             :must-read #{seon.sci.eval/call-detecting-program-change seon.turn/evaluate-sources}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/inv-lifecycle-storage-opus-2026-09-26.md"
                         "docs/seon/issues/an-evaluation-that-overlaps-a-reload-is-not-detected.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-blind-cross-branch-writer-is-promoted-to-a-read"
             :kind :function
             :intent "read-only-evaluation? (fixed for writers that read other branches in 90c542fd0) still promotes a form that writes another branch without reading it and reads only its own database, so a system turn may re-run a writer. A positive proof records every transaction the evaluation made."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/read-only-evaluation?}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/fix-root-graph-panic-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "failed-run-left-unsettled"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A run failing after it records its sources was never closed with its cause (sorted-map wedge, sticky unavailable). rc-m4 f83931226 closes escaping runs; proven by root after restart."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/turn}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/agent-path-2-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "late-faults-accompany-evaluated-values"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A settlement refusal, a renderer failure or a printing failure never replaces a completed evaluation's value: it is saved beside it as :seon.cluster.eval/diagnostics and shown as its own response entry; each settlement saves its fault window as references."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/refusal-terminal-data seon.turn/settle-batch-refusal! seon.turn/fault-window}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/error-delivery-audit-2026-09-26.md"
                         "docs/research/agent-platform/landing/err-s12-record-data-opus-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-turn-pass-derives-its-work-twice"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Each agent pass derives next-agent-work on a fresh database value, runs the turn, then derives it again for the rewake (more-agent-work?); with episode-exhausted! (turns-left: episode-runs + max-episode-runs, four history queries) and opening-deferred? a trivial form spends ~12 ms and ~59 seon.db/q in turn derivations (2 ms for the same derivation on a held value). The rewake answer is decidable from the pass's own report (a closed run with no unanswered wake) without re-deriving from scratch; the budget reads scan the agent's whole turn history since assignment."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/step seon.turn/next-agent-work seon.turn/more-agent-work? seon.turn/turns-left}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/perf-eval-overhead-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-agent-form-is-read-again-by-each-consumer"
             :kind :function
             :intent "After the plan reads a reply, its source is read again by evaluable-source?, read-source, source-events (source-key and read-only-evaluation?, which also re-derives declarations with program/declaration-row), seon.sci.eval/one-event and install-row!'s test and deletion replays, besides the clj-kondo parse. A form is parsed once: carry the plan's events and the analyzed entities on the evaluation."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/source-events seon.turn/read-only-evaluation? seon.turn/evaluable-source? seon.sci.eval/one-event}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/eval-path-audit-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "receipt-attributes-are-a-hand-list"
             :kind :function
             :intent "seon.turn/receipt-terminal-attributes is a hand-written list of the receipt attributes the writer stores; it duplicates the receipt schema, so every new receipt key is silently dropped until someone edits the list (the LARGE VALUE cost keys of 2026-09-27 never landed because of it). Derive the stored attributes from the receipt's declared schema (CLAUDE.md: declare the partition on entity schemas, not in a hand list) and delete the list."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/receipt-terminal-attributes}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/large-value-feedback-opus-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "opening-run-id-from-a-stale-count"
             :kind :function
             :intent "seon.turn/system-turn derives an opening's run id from a count of the agent's runs on its own connection read, so competing openers agree on one id and the writer admits one. Under reply-when-applied (B) an agent's answered run may not be committed yet, so an opening started inside that window can compute an id that collides with it; the writer refuses loudly (no corruption), but the opening fails. submit-source! was fixed by generating ids (5151be7ad); openings need a collision-free identity that still lets competing openers converge (e.g. derive the id from the triggering fact's identity, not a count)."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/system-turn}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/one-commit-a-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-gate-exception-refuses-the-whole-run"
             :kind :function
             :intent "An exception inside the install gate escapes as a phase failure (turn.clj ~3551-3567), lands on ordinal 0 and leaves every other ordinal nil: the whole run is refused instead of its one form, violating the total REPL path. The failing form gets its diagnostic beside its value; the others keep theirs (probe-i, cc91a1e62). Worse: turn/phase (turn.clj:3551) catches only ExceptionInfo, so any other throwable (an NPE, fault 8f6fdfc0) escapes the turn step to Flow and the caller gets \"The awaited channel closed before completion\" with no value at all (fix-b, 5fffb1b48)."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/evaluate-sources}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/program-test-candidate-projection-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "analyze-settlement-is-unreached"
             :kind :function
             :intent "seon.turn/analyze-settlement re-analyzes a settled program entity outside SCI, so it cannot see declared-without-root Vars and drops their call edges (the gap b3f1a7ae7 fixed in sci.eval/analyzed-rows). It appears unreached in production: every settle! call site passes :seon.error/value, ordinary runs settle through receipt-settle-batch-tx which does not re-analyze, and its only database-arity caller with a program entity is test/seon/fn_test.clj:544. Verify no caller, then delete it and that test (fix-declare-edges, b3f1a7ae7)."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/analyze-settlement}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/program-test-candidate-projection-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-nested-in-ns-loses-the-whole-run"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Probed on disposable branch p0-probe 2026-09-27: the run \"(do (in-ns 'p0.nested) 1) (+ 1 2)\" writes the second form's :seon.cluster.eval/ns as the lookup ref [:seon.ns/name p0.nested], but namespace-rows (turn.clj ~619) creates entities only for namespaces segmentation saw (top-level in-ns), so the settlement transaction is refused ('Nothing found for entity id', logged unbounded twice by Datahike) and the caller gets 'The awaited channel closed before completion' with no run id, no values, no events. Total-path violation. Every namespace an evaluation actually ran in or ended in is upserted as {:seon.ns/name sym} in the same settlement transaction (the owner's session-entity plan P2a, docs/research/agent-platform/repl-session-entity-design-2026-09-27.md problem 3)."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/evaluate-sources}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/repl-session-entity-design-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "history-acquisition-decodes-stored-read-plans"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Loading an agent's history (seon.eval/of-agent for the prompt, seon.turn/latest-evaluations for each system turn's refresh) takes 1.5-1.8 s for root's 117 evaluations versus 3 ms for a raw pull: seon.db/pull decodes stored :datahike.read/dependency-plan strings (17.6 M chars, up to 1.5 MB each, mostly generated reads such as seon.error/for-agent) that no render shows. Context cost must follow what is shown; store bounded plans and pull none for display (data-model plan slice P1)."
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/repl-session-entity-design-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "compaction-erases-no-history-form-text"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Form :seon.cluster.eval/source, comment, output, diagnostics and the turn's reply are declared :db/noHistory, so retracting a form at compaction erases its text permanently, contradicting the owner's ruling that compaction deletes nothing (history answers what was dropped). These attributes are write-once; remove the no-history flag before compaction retracts forms (data-model plan)."
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/repl-session-entity-design-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-refused-multi-form-settlement-returns-nothing"
             :kind :function
             :intent "The settlement of a refused multi-form run is recorded correctly (settle-batch-refusal! commits every receipt with its value, the refusal as its error and the fault), but seon.cluster.agent/evaluate-source! (agent.clj ~806-818) answers an opened run with only {:seon.turn/id :seon.db/db :seon.db/durable}, dropping :seon.turn.loop/refusal and the error outcome; direct callers get no error (the MCP bridge reads run-observation). Keep the refusal and outcome in the answer beside the run id (register-anywhere diagnosis db8937e8d)."
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/register-anywhere-diagnosis-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "schema-redeclaration-revalidates-unchanged-attributes"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Re-declaring an entity schema marks every one of its attributes affected (turn.clj ~1128-1133), so adoption re-validates all stored values of attributes whose declaration did not change; the work grows with history (a receipt re-declaration touches 43 attributes, 2,063 datoms, ~168K chars). Pass only attributes whose form changed; cost follows the change (fix-adoption-vector, bd3d35b19)."
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/register-anywhere-diagnosis-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-unbound-check-walks-the-live-value-without-a-bound"
             :kind :function
             :intent "unbound-value? walks the evaluation's live value with (tree-seq coll? seq value) after disarm: no bound, no deadline, no interrupt, and it ran twice per form (in the since-deleted problem routing and in evaluation-facts). An evaluation returning (range) realized 115.9 M elements (10.2 GB, 93.7% of heap) and killed default pid 63809 with OutOfMemoryError; a finite (map f (range 200000)) is realized in full although 32 are shown. Answer 'contains SCI's unbound marker' from admission's one bounded walk inside the arm (its object-node leaves) and delete the live-value walk at both callers. Class regression: an ordinary run of (range) settles and old gen stays flat."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/unbound-value? seon.turn/evaluation-facts seon.sci.admit/admit}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/oom-63809-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-refused-refusal-settlement-throws-past-the-reply"
             :kind :function
             :intent "settle-batch-refusal! throws 'Batch refusal settlement was refused.' when its own write is refused (turn.clj ~4165); the pass rethrows through close-escaped-run! (turn.clj ~5728), turn-step's finally closes the reply, and evaluate-source! answers :seon.await/closed-error (agent.clj ~826). The caller sees 'awaited channel closed' while the fault (317111871b50, 3 occurrences 16:17-17:51Z 2026-09-27) goes only to root and to the agent's NEXT run. The nested in-ns trigger is fixed (9e61340b2); the throw path remains. Return both refusals as the value ({:refused-outcome :refusal-refused}) and answer them on the reply; boot recovery closes the run as for any failed terminal write. Related: a-refused-multi-form-settlement-returns-nothing (the non-throwing arm). Done when a regression whose refusal settlement is itself refused gets both refusals as data in its own reply, never a closed channel."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/settle-batch-refusal! seon.cluster.agent/evaluate-source!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/stability-sweep-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-transient-write-refusal-parks-the-agent-until-restart"
             :kind :function
             :intent "At 02:35Z 2026-09-27 memory pressure refused root's renderer three times; the write-refusal bound parked root's turn proc (fault c2268b2f) until the JVM died, and every submission was answered by closing its reply (turn.clj ~6044-6051; park recorded ~6155-6160). A park is left only by disarm/arm: right for a deterministic storm, wrong for a transient cause that clears. Neither fault was messaged (root is removed from its own recipients, error.clj ~1516-1520), and a parked root builds no next context. Keep wakes suppressed while parked, but give an explicit submission one attempt, answer its refusal on the reply, and clear the park on a committed write. Done when a regression that parks an agent through a transient refusal then submits after the cause clears gets an answered run, and a still-refused submission gets the park error on its reply."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/step seon.turn/write-refusal-bound seon.turn/record-write-refusal-fault!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/stability-sweep-2026-09-27.md"
                         "docs/seon/issues/a-parked-turn-proc-pings-unknown-on-a-healthy-agent.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-definition-is-settled-twice"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A definition's facts are derived twice: once on the batch's working value, where the writer's validation admits them form by form (seon.turn.batch/apply-facts), then again at the writer on the head in the turn's one settlement (seon.turn.batch/program-facts-call, since S4a; before it, with-settled then receipt-settle-call's row-tx: x43 mean 315 ms and x93 mean 244 ms on pid 66629, the second on the writer thread where it serializes every write on the branch). Settle the working value's validated tx-data under an expected-basis fence (:datahike/expected-basis-t) and re-derive only when the head moved. Done when a defining form's facts are derived once (profile count per form) and a moved head still settles correctly."
             :must-read #{seon.turn.batch/program-facts-call seon.turn.batch/apply-facts seon.turn/settle-batch!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/perf-audit-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-trivial-agent-turn-takes-eleven-seconds"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A Seon agent turn whose scripted reply is two cheap forms took about 11 s from its wake message to its close on default (e999fdd1b, local stub provider, 2026-09-27): message tx 22:19:34.2 -> turn opened 22:19:39.6 (5.4 s) -> provider request 22:19:45 (5.4 s) -> closed 22:19:45.7; the same forms through MCP SCI answer in 13-112 ms. Five turns measured 1.9-9.1 s open-to-close. Name the armed functions in the wake-to-open and open-to-request windows (docs/seon/issues/a-turn-spends-seconds-before-its-provider-call.md is the earlier record) and make an ordinary turn's overhead sub-second. Profiled 2026-09-27 (one-path lane, default, root's 146 evaluations): wake-to-open is seon.turn/system-turn, 3.8-5.2 s, of which seon.turn/latest-evaluations is 3,566 ms (the same query without the read-evidence plans: 12 ms) -> seon.db/with-declarations x237 -> seon.schema.datahike/decode-attribute-value-in x996 3,694 ms decoding 477 stored :datahike.read/dependency-plan values (36.5 MB, the largest 4,536,371 chars from the opening's read (seon.error/for-agent (seon.db/db) \"root\"), whose :datahike.query.dependency/sources holds one whole vector). Open-to-request is the cold seon.cluster.prompt/prompt, 7,614 ms (warm 49 ms): seon.render.walk/history 7,385 ms = seon.eval/of-agent 3,693 ms (the same plan decode) + seon.db/call-reach x143 3,097 ms from seon.render/render-program-evidence (one reach per rendered value, not per distinct renderer). One target: 300 ms overhead; the proof is tmp/one-path one_path_proof.clj :turn-overhead. Diagnosed 2026-09-27 (turn-overhead lane, tmp/turn-overhead/proof.clj): the 4.5 M-char plan is not a copied vector but 30,729 :seon.db/read-index-patterns, one per datom the read's pull-in-find reached (seon.db/pull-index-patterns since ed8fd02e3, reached by the opening since fef94ab7a); 32 of 477 stored plans exceed 16 KB, all this read, 36.1 of 36.5 M chars. Landed or routed: the capture keeps at most 128 patterns and the schema refuses more (2.59 M -> 114 chars, same freshness and generated-read answers); latest-evaluations decodes the kept evaluations' evidence only (3.7 s -> 0.48 s, same value); history pulls evidence as references (of-agent 3.8 s -> 14 ms, same text); call reach keyed for the as-of opening (x147 2.75 s -> 19 ms); the walk's pull lowering cached per projection and selector (x63 1.16 s -> 36 ms). Remaining after install: re-measure a real turn; the cold history render of every evaluation (a-cold-prompt-renders-every-evaluation-again) and the preview of changed reads (~0.3 s)."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/turn-step seon.turn/system-turn seon.turn/latest-evaluations seon.eval/of-agent seon.render/render-program-evidence}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/repl-dogfood-2026-09-27.md" "tmp/turn-overhead/proof.clj"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-mcp-run-of-root-waits-behind-roots-faulting-system-turn"
             :kind :function
             :intent "On default (pid 85484, 52e163bee, 2026-09-28) a root SCI run submitted through seon.cluster.agent/evaluate-source! answers in 88-114 ms when root is idle, but a second run submitted right after answers in 6.5-7.0 s (four back-to-back runs: 152, 7046, 6551, 6586 ms; after a 10 s pause 114 ms). The settled run wakes root's system turn (seon.turn/system-turn), which runs about 6.8 s and ends in the fault 'A generated context read depends on the agent's own turn-taking.' (signature 86621df3f8c7..., occurrences 9 -> 21 during the probes), and the next submission waits for it on root's request port. Every MCP call as root pays it once root is busy. Done when a run submitted right after another answers within 200 ms of an idle one and a settled run records no generated-read fault."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/system-turn seon.turn/generated-read-fault seon.cluster.agent/evaluate-source!}
             :evidence #{"tmp/mcp-entrance/timings.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "row-tx-is-a-second-declaration-writer"
             :kind :function
             :intent "seon.turn/row-tx has no production caller since the fold writes declarations through seon.turn.batch/entity-facts and schema-tx (its concurrency check and schema attribute-change copy went in the writer-duplicates cut). Test fixtures in ten files still write declarations through it (datahike_fork, db, schema, schema_usage_guard, cluster/acquisition, program, sci/branch_execution, sci/eval, schema/datahike, schema/projection_writer). Done when those fixtures write through the fold's writer and row-tx with its declared-* helpers is deleted."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/row-tx seon.turn.batch/form-facts}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/one-repl-path-spec-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "eval-origin-has-no-producer-or-consumer"
             :kind :namespace
             :intent ":seon.eval/origin has no producer and no consumer (about fifteen turn.clj sites, e.g. turn.clj:116-117, 848) and Astra's seon.db/retention-check issue-authority code (db.clj:5470) is dead beside it. Done when both are deleted with their schema entries and callers converted in one commit."
             :must-read #{seon.db/retention-check}}
 #:seon.task{:name "turn-continue-read-results-arrive-before-done-takes-six-seconds"
             :kind :namespace
             :intent "turn-continue read-results-arrive-before-done (:147) takes 5.8-7.3 s: undiagnosed. Done when a profile names the responsible armed functions and the test fits its bound."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/compact!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-deep-value-loses-the-run"
             :kind :function
             :intent "An MCP sci run of (reduce (fn [v _] [v]) [] (range 10000)) on a branch agent (default pid 30874, 2026-10-07, waste-rank lane) evaluates in 34-47 ms, then its settlement is refused 'run transition refused: no-terminal-fact' (seon.turn/receipt-settle-call, validator :seon.turn/refused-error) and the reply carries only that 1,138-character diagnostic: the agent never receives its value (218-314 ms, 357-452 MB, 81-122 k armed calls, transact! x4). The one path is not total for deep values. Hypothesis to verify first: a recursive walk in recording or settlement (admission's project, the shown map or the receipt validation) overflows the stack and the throw becomes the refusal. Done when the case returns its value with a bounded shown text and settles as an ordinary evaluation; regression: that form through the one path, asserting the reply's value handle and the settled receipt."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/receipt-settle-call seon.sci.admit/admit seon.sci.eval/shown-result}
             :evidence #{"tmp/waste-rank/large-values.edn" "tmp/waste-rank/error-replies.txt"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "each-trivial-evaluation-retains-two-hundred-kilobytes"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Heap after System/gc grows linearly with ordinary evaluations: 100 MCP runs of (+ 1 2) on one branch agent grew default's live heap 2,562 -> 2,582 MB (~200 KB per run), and a further 100 runs added 22.9 MB in the live class histogram: Object[] +10.2 MB, datahike.datom.Datom +60,174 (about 600 per run for a 28-datom commit), persistent-sorted-set Leaf +6,296 and Branch +2,531 (default pid 30874, 2026-10-07, tmp/waste-rank/hist-diff-100-evals.txt). Retiring the branch did not release its heap within 3 s. Hypothesis: something keeps each commit's database value reachable (read evidence, a reply's basis, a world or cache key holding a db), pinning old index leaves. Find the holder with a heap dump's path to GC roots for the new Datoms and drop the reference at its producer (hold a commit id, never a db value). Done when 100 x (+ 1 2) leaves the live heap within noise (< 1 MB) and a retired branch's context and results are collectable; regression: the 100-run heap probe as a test with a declared memory bound."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/settle-batch! seon.batch/freeze-step seon.cluster.agent/release-context!}
             :evidence #{"tmp/waste-rank/hist-diff-100-evals.txt" "tmp/waste-rank/class-histogram-live.txt"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "read-evidence-rows-are-two-fifths-of-the-stored-bytes"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Each evaluation's database reads are stored as one vector cell plus one evidence entity per read (receipt-read-evidence-tx), with :datahike.read/dependency-plan and :datahike.read/revision as printed strings (~660 bytes per read; 712 distinct revision strings repeated over 23,662 rows). On default (pid 30874, basis 536872613, 2026-10-07, db-watch lane) that is 23,663 reads owned by 115 of 1,189 evaluations: ~142k datoms (19% of 762k current datoms) and 15.7 MB of the 39 MB of stored value bytes (41%); four prepare-merge! evaluations hold 2,259 reads each (~1.5 MB per evaluation) and a probe defn ~547. The only reader is the old loop's refresh before a system turn (seon.turn/latest-evaluations, refresh status at the read-evidence-current? check), which the agent-loop spec deletes with system turns, while its settlement section keeps writing the rows. Evaluation results are runtime only (owner 2026-10-07), so evidence for re-evaluating them has no consumer after the cut. Done when settlement writes no read-evidence rows (or a bounded digest per evaluation, if a named consumer survives the cut), the attribute counts in docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/db-watch/watch.clj stop growing with reads, and a regression asserts a 2,000-read evaluation settles under 10 datoms of evidence."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/receipt-read-evidence-tx seon.turn/latest-evaluations seon.db/read-evidence-current?}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/db-watch/findings.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-deferred-wake-is-answered-by-silence",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "When `seon.turn/opening-deferred?` is true for a new wake, `next-agent-work` derives nil and nothing is recorded or delivered: the sender gets no reply, no fault, no diagnostic (agent r-turn-a, message ee63e682b520, default 2026-10-07). `episode-exhausted!` fires only on the pass that spends the last run, never for a wake arriving after. A wake the bound defers must reach the sender (or root) as data. Proof: a deftest that messages an exhausted agent and asserts the deferral is a stored, delivered error value.",
            :must-read
            #{seon.turn/next-agent-work seon.turn/episode-exhausted!},
            :evidence
            #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/plan-research/r-turn-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-markdown-heading-in-a-reply-is-read-as-code",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "planned-sources keeps Markdown structure as source: (planned-sources \"The fix is in `data-text`.\\n## What\\n(+ 1 2)\" 'user 100000) answers one source \"## What\\n(+ 1 2)\" (2026-10-07, default at 42e569d9d), whose `##` is an invalid reader token; live run 1 also saw backticked prose evaluated as syntax-quote. Prose lines, Markdown headings and fenced/backticked spans become comments; only forms are evaluated.",
            :must-read #{seon.turn/planned-sources},
            :evidence
            #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/live-run/report.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-message-from-root-never-refills-the-turn-bound",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "A message root sends an agent is an inside wake, so `seon.turn/outside-wake-t` stays 0 and `seon.turn/episode-runs` counts every turn the agent ever took. On default 2026-10-07, agent r-turn-a (branch :agent-cfd70b5547f7) with :seon.config.run/max-episode-runs 1 and one spent turn received root's message ee63e682b520 (seon.cluster.message/send! ... conn \"root\"): next-agent-work answered nil, outside-wake-t 0, spent 1, max 1, unanswered-wakes held the message. Root's work requests must refill the bound (or the bound must count calls per wake). Proof: a deftest where root messages an agent whose spent runs equal the dial and the agent takes one turn.",
            :must-read
            #{seon.turn/opening-deferred? seon.turn/outside-wake-t},
            :evidence
            #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/plan-research/r-turn-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-prose-only-reply-evaluates-its-first-word",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "When planned-sources finds no form (a prose-only model reply), the turn's freeze step plans the whole reply text as one row with no reader event, and read-row reads only that row's FIRST form as code: 'I will add two numbers.' evaluates the symbol I (Unable to resolve symbol) and the rest of the row is dropped unread; live run call 11 answered 'Invalid token: ##What' for its heading. The no-forms refusal ('Your reply had no form; only comments/prose. Send a form.') that planned-sources built never reaches the agent. Reproduce (default 722f110df): (seon.turn/planned-sources \"I will add two numbers.\" 'user 1048576) is the no-forms error; seon.turn/read-row over a row with that source reads I with end 1 of 23. Correct: the no-forms refusal is the row's read error, delivered once, and no prose is evaluated. Proof: a turn with a fixture reply of prose only answers the no-forms refusal as data.",
            :must-read #{seon.turn/read-row seon.turn/planned-sources},
            :evidence
            #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/plan-research/r-read-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name
            "generated-opening-reads-fail-when-evaluated-as-written",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "The system turn's generated opening reads (seon.turn/declared-sources) include forms that fail when an agent evaluates the same text: (dir (quote user)) refuses 'seon.sci.eval/directory-value refused argument 1 namespace-name: expected a symbol, got a sequence', and (seon.db/pull (quote [*]) [:seon.runtime/agent {:seon.agent/id \"r-context-probe\", :db/id 125969}]) answers 'Expected number or lookup ref for entity id' (the lookup value is a pulled map, not an identity). In lp-add-2's captured prompt the second showed as a value holding only :seon.render/operations, so the failure was hidden from the model. Measured as agent r-context-probe on default (722f110df, 2026-10-07). Done when every generated opening form evaluates to the value it is shown with, or the system turn is deleted (live-agents plan D1).",
            :must-read
            #{seon.turn/declared-sources seon.turn/unit-read},
            :evidence
            #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/plan-research/r-context-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "generated-render-sources-are-read-as-model-replies",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "seon.turn's system plan segments each render block's generated :seon.render/source with seon.turn/planned-sources, the model-reply reader with prose forgiveness, instead of seon.turn/code-sources: system-generated code is read as if a model wrote it (turn.clj near the unit-read reduce in HEAD 439401ac3). Correct: generated code is code (code-sources).",
            :must-read
            #{seon.turn/code-sources seon.turn/planned-sources}}
 #:seon.task{:name "each-form-of-a-pass-costs-megabytes"
             :kind :function
             :intent "An MCP SCI pass costs megabytes per form whatever the form does. Measured on default with seon.pass-probe/mcp!: 100 forms (+ n 1) 469 ms / 1.15 GB; 400 unresolved words 1,481 ms / 2.37 GB. After pass-cost (once-per-pass namespace lookup, config defaults, terminal ordinal, identity pull, JVM render policy): 100 forms SCI 191 ms / 350 MB, JVM 115 ms / 195 MB, still about 2.3 MB per SCI form. Remaining: retired context refresh and read evidence (S4), repeated recording and observation (S5), receipt machinery (S6), armed hot internals, Datahike query-cache inheritance walk. Correct: per-form work proportional to what the form did, well under 1 MB for a form that defines nothing. Proof: a pass-probe row where 100 non-defining forms allocate under 10x one form."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/resume-turn seon.db/with-declarations}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/pass-cost/design-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-armed-agents-run-forks-the-program-acquired-before-a-later-commit"
             :kind :function
             :intent "An armed agent's run (resume-turn) forked the cluster base context it captured at arming without acquiring the program its connection now holds, so a program change committed by another writer (boot's adoption of the loaded files, another agent's definition) stayed invisible to every SCI run until something else called seon.cluster.agent/acquire-context!. Observed on default after a restart onto b5ff51e4c: root's (doc h) failed 'Unable to resolve symbol: seon.sci.eval/reference-in' for two minutes while a freshly hosted agent and the JVM path saw the function. Correct: each pass acquires its base at the connection's current value before forking (a no-op when the program is unchanged)."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/resume-turn seon.sci.eval/acquire! seon.cluster.agent/acquire-context!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/doc-fix/doc-resolution-notes.md"}}]
